      "text": "We start with the given functional equation:\n\\[\nf(x) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 4^x.\n\\]\nFirst, we substitute \\(x\\) with \\(\\frac{1}{x}\\) in the original equation. This gives us:\n\\[\nf \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) - 2 f(x) = 4^{\\frac{1}{x}}.\n\\]\nNow we have a system of two equations:\n1. \\(f(x) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 4^x\\),\n2. \\(f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) - 2 f(x) = 4^{\\frac{1}{x}}\\).\n\nWe can solve this system by expressing \\(f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right)\\) from the second equation and substituting it into the first equation. From the second equation, we have:\n\\[\nf \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 2 f(x) + 4^{\\frac{1}{x}}.\n\\]\nSubstitute this expression for \\(f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right)\\) into the first equation:\n\\[\nf(x) - 2 \\left( 2 f(x) + 4^{\\frac{1}{x}} \\right) = 4^x.\n\\]\nSimplify the left side:\n\\[\nf(x) - 4 f(x) - 2 \\cdot 4^{\\frac{1}{x}} = 4^x,\n\\]\n\\[\n-3 f(x) - 2 \\cdot 4^{\\frac{1}{x}} = 4^x.\n\\]\nRearrange to solve for \\(f(x)\\):\n\\[\n-3 f(x) = 4^x + 2 \\cdot 4^{\\frac{1}{x}},\n\\]\n\\[\nf(x) = -\\frac{4^x + 2 \\cdot 4^{\\frac{1}{x}}}{3}.\n\\]\nWe need to find \\(f(2)\\). Substitute \\(x = 2\\) into the expression for \\(f(x)\\):\n\\[\nf(2) = -\\frac{4^2 + 2 \\cdot 4^{\\frac{1}{2}}}{3} = -\\frac{16 + 2 \\cdot 2}{3} = -\\frac{16 + 4}{3} = -\\frac{20}{3}.\n\\]\nTherefore, the value of \\(f(2)\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\frac{20}{3}}.\n\\]",
