      "text": "To solve for \\( f(2) \\), we start by analyzing the given functional equation:\n\\[ f(x) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 4^x. \\]\n\nFirst, we substitute \\( x = 2 \\) into the equation:\n\\[ f(2) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = 4^2 = 16. \\]\nThis gives us the first equation:\n\\[ f(2) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = 16. \\tag{1} \\]\n\nNext, we substitute \\( x = \\frac{1}{2} \\) into the original equation:\n\\[ f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) - 2 f(2) = 4^{\\frac{1}{2}}. \\]\nSince \\( 4^{\\frac{1}{2}} = 2 \\), we have:\n\\[ f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) - 2 f(2) = 2. \\]\nThis gives us the second equation:\n\\[ f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) - 2 f(2) = 2. \\tag{2} \\]\n\nNow we have a system of linear equations:\n1. \\( f(2) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = 16, \\)\n2. \\( f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) - 2 f(2) = 2. \\)\n\nTo eliminate \\( f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) \\), we can multiply the second equation by 2:\n\\[ 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) - 4 f(2) = 4. \\tag{3} \\]\n\nNow we add equations (1) and (3):\n\\[ (f(2) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right)) + (2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) - 4 f(2)) = 16 + 4. \\]\nSimplifying the left side, we get:\n\\[ f(2) - 4 f(2) = 20, \\]\n\\[ -3 f(2) = 20, \\]\n\\[ f(2) = -\\frac{20}{3}. \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( f(2) \\) is \\(\\boxed{-\\frac{20}{3}}\\).",
